  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...\nHigh pressure and upper level ridging will result in mostly\nsunny skies and above average temperatures into Sunday with\nhighs generally in the 60s. For reference, record-highs this \ntime of the year remain fairly elevated in the lower 70s. \n\nBy late Sunday, an upper-level trough will extend a cold front \neast through the area. Some precipitation in the form of rain \nis expected along the front, especially Sunday evening and \nSunday night, though the amounts will be light. Behind the\nfront, a brief push of colder air will enter the region on \nMonday into Tuesday, characterized by 850 mb temperatures \nfalling to around -10 degrees C Monday night. There is a small \nwindow for lake effect precipitation during this timeframe, and\nperhaps even some instances of light lake effect snow, though \nan upper level ridge and high pressure will quickly build into \nthe region by late Tuesday, ending any residual threat for \nprecipitation. Temperatures will remain seasonably- cool behind \nthe front Monday through Wednesday with highs in the upper 30s \nto lower 40s.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2... \nThe latest guidance continues to suggest the development of a\ncoastal low moving north along the Southeast and Mid-Atlantic\nCoasts late Tuesday into Friday. At this time, the greatest\nimpacts are expected well to the east of the area, though some \nprecipitation on the western periphery of the system in the form\nof rain or a rain/snow mix is possible, especially Wednesday\nnight into Thanksgiving day. Temperatures will remain seasonably-\ncool during mid to late next week with highs in the upper 30s \nto lower 40s.",
